{% doc %}
  A background image that fills its container.

  ## Not a CSS background

  This renders a real `<img>` rather than `background-image`, for three reasons
  that all matter more than the one line of CSS it saves:

    - `srcset` works. A CSS background downloads one file at one size to every
      device; an `<img>` lets the browser pick, which is the difference between
      a 200KB and a 900KB hero on a phone.
    - `loading` and `fetchpriority` work, so a below-the-fold background is
      lazy and an above-the-fold one is not.
    - It goes through `snippets/image.liquid` like every other image in the
      theme, so the width/height attributes that keep cumulative layout shift at
      zero are applied in one place rather than remembered here.

  ## Cover or fit

  `cover` crops to fill — the right default for a decorative backdrop, where
  losing the edges costs nothing. `fit` shows the whole image and lets the
  container's background show around it, which is what a logo or a product
  cut-out needs.

  Focal point is honoured in both cases: Shopify's `image.presentation.focal_point`
  is where the merchant said the subject is, so a crop keeps it in frame instead
  of centring blindly and cutting a head off.

  ## Parallax

  Pass `parallax: true` and the wrapper becomes `<motion-effect
  data-effect="parallax">`, the theme's existing motion element — no new
  component, no second scroll listener. `assets/motion-engine.js` already runs
  one shared ticker that every parallax on the page subscribes to.

  The engine clamps its offset to ±120px, so the layer is given 120px of
  overscan above and below in `base.css`. Without it a background moved 120px up
  leaves 120px of empty container at the bottom — the usual parallax bug, and
  the reason it is worth doing here rather than in each section.

  The engine sits out on touch devices and under reduced motion, so the overscan
  is behind a media query matching the same conditions. Nothing is oversized on
  a phone, where nothing would move anyway.

  ## Speed is a percentage here and a fraction in the engine

  The schema stores 5–40 with a `%` unit, because "20%" is a strength a merchant
  can reason about. `parallax()` wants a multiplier — 0.2 — and hands the result
  to a ±120px clamp.

  Passing the percentage straight through is what made parallax *jump*: at
  `speed: 25` every offset in normal scroll range lands outside the clamp, so
  the transform snaps between exactly −120px and +120px with nothing in between.
  It looked like the effect was broken; it was running perfectly on a value 100×
  too large. The conversion happens here, once.

  @param {image} image
  @param {image} [mobile_image] - Art direction below 767px
  @param {string} [position] - cover | fit. Default cover.
  @param {boolean} [parallax] - Drift on scroll. Ignored on touch and reduced motion.
  @param {number} [speed] - Strength as a percentage, 5–40. Default 15.
  @param {boolean} [priority] - Eager load. Above-the-fold backgrounds only.
  @param {string} [classes] - Extra classes on the wrapper.

  @example
    {% render 'background-image', image: block.settings.image,
       position: block.settings.image_position %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign fit = position | default: 'cover'

  assign use_parallax = false
  if parallax
    assign use_parallax = true
  endif

  # The element differs, the classes do not, so the rest of the stylesheet does
  # not need to know which one it got.
  # Percentage in, multiplier out. Clamped first so a stored value from an older
  # schema cannot reach the engine unconverted — that is the failure this had.
  assign speed_pct = speed | default: 15 | plus: 0
  if speed_pct < 5
    assign speed_pct = 5
  endif
  if speed_pct > 40
    assign speed_pct = 40
  endif
  assign speed_value = speed_pct | divided_by: 100.0

  if use_parallax
    assign tag = 'motion-effect'
    assign layer_classes = 'background-media__layer background-media__layer--parallax'
  else
    assign tag = 'div'
    assign layer_classes = 'background-media__layer'
  endif
  assign layer_classes = layer_classes | append: ' background-media__layer--' | append: fit

  # `default` rather than a blank test: an unset focal point is nil in Shopify
  # and Undefined elsewhere, and comparing either to `blank` is engine-specific.
  # `default` replaces nil, false and empty string in every engine.
  assign focal = image.presentation.focal_point | default: '50% 50%'
-%}

{%- if image -%}
  <{{ tag }}
    class="{{ layer_classes }} {{ classes }}"
    style="--background-focal: {{ focal }};"
    {%- if use_parallax %}
      data-effect="parallax"
      data-speed="{{ speed_value }}"
      data-axis="y"
    {%- endif %}
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      `alt: ''` deliberately. A background is decoration: the section's own
      heading carries the meaning, and announcing the filename after it is noise
      to a screen reader rather than information.

      `sizes: '100vw'` because the container is as wide as whatever holds it and
      Liquid cannot know that. Guessing narrower would ship a blurry image.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% render 'image',
       image: image,
       mobile_image: mobile_image,
       alt: '',
       ratio: 'none',
       sizes: '100vw',
       mobile_sizes: '100vw',
       priority: priority,
       class: 'background-media__image' %}
  </{{ tag }}>
{%- endif -%}
